# Learn more https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/getting-started-with-git/ignoring-files

# dependencies
node_modules/

# Expo
.expo/
dist/
web-build/
expo-env.d.ts

# Native
.kotlin/
*.orig.*
*.jks
*.p8
*.p12
*.key
*.mobileprovision

# Metro
.metro-health-check*

# debug
npm-debug.*
yarn-debug.*
yarn-error.*

# macOS
.DS_Store
*.pem

# local env files
.env*.local

# ── Deployment and provider credentials ──────────────────────────────────────
# `.env.deploy` holds the Neon connection string, the JWT signing key, the seed
# password and the SMS provider keys. `.env*.local` above does not match it, so
# it was one `git add -A` away from being committed to a repository that is
# private today and may not always be.
#
# Anything holding a live credential goes here, not in a pattern that happens to
# cover the filename somebody chose this week.
.env.deploy
.env.deploy.*

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo

# test output
coverage/

# generated native folders
/ios
/android

# ── Firebase / push credentials ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# The service-account key is a live credential: it can send push as this project
# to anyone holding it. It belongs in a secret store, never in the tree.
**/*firebase-adminsdk*.json
*service-account*.json

# `google-services.json` is not secret — it ships inside the APK and its API key
# is restricted by package name and signing certificate — but it is
# environment-specific, and committing one project's file makes every other
# environment build against it silently.
#
# If CI needs to build without fetching it from a secret store, delete these two
# lines; that is a deliberate choice rather than an oversight.
google-services.json
android/app/google-services.json

# ── Runtime probe output ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `evidence-*.json` and `results-*.json` are the recorders' raw transcripts:
# every request and response, which means live bearer tokens and the shared
# fixture password. They are regenerated by any probe run and are worth nothing
# to a reader of the repository.
#
# The curated evidence that *is* worth keeping lives under `docs/` and carries
# no credentials.
runtime-validation/**/evidence-*.json
runtime-validation/**/results-*.json

# ── Documentation is not committed ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Reports, matrices, plans and phase write-ups stay local.
#
# `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` are deliberately excluded from this rule below:
# they are not documentation but the repository's own instructions, and
# `AGENTS.md` carries the constraint that Expo's versioned docs must be read
# before any code is written here. Dropping them would remove a working rule.
*.md
!CLAUDE.md
!AGENTS.md
# The release verdict. Tracked so the decision survives a clone even for somebody
# who never fetches `docs/`.
!ANDROID-UAT-VERDICT.md

# ── The release record has to survive a clone ───────────────────────────────
#
# This was a flat `docs/`, so every UAT deliverable this programme produced —
# two sessions, fifty-odd findings, the reproductions and the evidence — existed
# only on the machine that ran the tests (UAT-115). A verdict nobody else can
# read is not a verdict.
#
# Note the shape. Git **cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is
# excluded**, so `docs/` followed by `!docs/uat/...` does nothing at all: the
# directory is never descended into and the negations are never consulted. Each
# level has to be opened before the next can be narrowed, which is why this is
# four lines instead of one.
#
# The written record is tracked; the screenshot evidence is tens of megabytes and
# stays out.
docs/*
!docs/uat/
docs/uat/*
!docs/uat/android/
docs/uat/android/*
!docs/uat/android/*.md
